<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/market-failure-externalities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Markets are extraordinary allocation machines — <strong>when prices tell the truth</strong>.
    Market failure is the study of the four big ways prices stop telling the truth:
    <strong>externalities, public goods, information asymmetry and monopoly power</strong>. Each
    failure is also the economic justification for some government role — which is why this
    lesson is where economics meets politics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classic failures — each a crack in the price signal" widget="equationStep" config={failCfg} />

  <h2>The fixes, and their own failures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Price the externality:</strong> carbon taxes and cap-and-trade make polluters pay — the market mechanism redirected, not replaced. Pigou (1920) proposed it; carbon pricing is his idea at planetary scale.</li>
    <li><strong>Government supplies public goods:</strong> funded by taxation, since voluntary payment fails. Coase (1960) showed property rights sometimes fix externalities privately — when negotiation is cheap.</li>
    <li><strong>Disclosure and regulation for asymmetry:</strong> food labels, car history reports, banking rules. Signalling and warranties rebuild trust markets need.</li>
    <li><strong>Government failure is real too:</strong> regulators get captured, subsidies misfire, incentives distort. The mature position is comparative: which institution fails worse <em>in this specific case</em>?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pigou (1920); Coase (1960); Akerlof, "The Market for Lemons" (1970); OpenStax, Principles of Economics.</p>
</TopicPage>
